#!/bin/sh
''''test "$(uname -s)" = Darwin && exec python3 "$0" "$@" # '''
''''command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 && python3 -c pass >/dev/null 2>&1 && exec python3 "$0" "$@" # '''
''''exec python "$0" "$@" # '''
__doc__ = """mission-control - run prepared autopilot sessions unattended, several at a time.

usage: mission-control run [--scheduled] | list | add <repo> <item> [<branch>] | labels [<repo>] | doctor
       mission-control status | stop [<item>] | retry <repo> <#pr | item> | log [<item>]
       mission-control start | pause [until <YYYY-MM-DD> | <N>d] | resume
       mission-control install-schedule [HH:MM | <N>m] | uninstall-schedule

One machine-wide queue under $MISSION_CONTROL_HOME (default ~/.claude/mission-control):
  queue.txt   one item per line: <repo path> <item> [<branch>]   (# comments; item = session
              dir, ticket key, or a "quoted topic"; the branch column exists for session dirs
              only, `add` fills it in); processed top to bottom
  repos.txt   one repo path per line; polled for open PRs labelled autopilot-ready
  done.txt    <ISO time> <repo> <item> <status> <pr url or -> [restarts=N] [no-plan] [labels-failed]
              [push-failed] [runner-error]
  held.txt    <repo> <pr number> <status> for a PR whose run ended while its autopilot-ready
              label could not be changed; it is not run again until the label changes or
              "retry" releases it
  env         optional, mode 600, KEY=VALUE lines: SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL and MISSION_CONTROL_* settings;
              a line starting with "export" also reaches the run's environment
  logs/       <timestamp>-s<slot>-<item>.log per item (PR items: <timestamp>-s<slot>-_<n>-<resolved
              item>.log, renamed once the session dir or ticket key is known), queue.log for
              lines outside an item
  worktrees/  where runs before plugin 3.3.0 kept their worktrees; one found here moves into
              the project on the item's next attempt
  run.lock/   pid and start time (started) of the active run and current-<slot> per item that
              is running (read by status), removed after the run
  edit.lock   taken for the moment a queue.txt or held.txt line is added or removed
  locks/      one lock file per repository: the git steps that touch a repository's shared
              .git (worktree add, fetch, push, worktree remove) run one at a time per repository
  paused      "until-resume", or one date (YYYY-MM-DD, local): the schedule is paused until
              resume, or through that day; the LaunchAgent's "run --scheduled" skips while it
              is valid, a manual run ignores it
  force-once  written by start; the scheduled run it starts consumes it and ignores the pause
              (it counts for five minutes; pause removes it)

Each item runs in its own worktree inside the project, <repo>/.claude/worktrees/
autopilot-<item>, where developers and the Claude app keep theirs and where the workbench
backup finds it. The runner adds .claude/worktrees/ to the repository's local exclude file
when the project does not ignore it. A done item's worktree is removed; any other keeps it
for a retry.

The run itself (claude -p "/autopilot <item>") pushes, opens or updates the PR and works
the review bot. The queue only starts runs, watches them, restarts on HANDOFF.md, reads the
first line of REPORT.md ("Status: done" or "Status: blocked - <reason>"), labels the PR with
the outcome and notifies.

One "run" process per machine (lock). It starts up to MISSION_CONTROL_SLOTS items at once
(default 2; MISSION_CONTROL_SLOTS_PER_REPO limits the items of one repository, default no
limit), each in a worker process of its own ("run-one", internal), and fills a slot again
the moment its item ends. While items run it checks queue.txt and the labelled PRs again
every MISSION_CONTROL_CHECK_MIN minutes (15), so work queued during a run starts within that
time. It ends when nothing runs and nothing waits. Each run gets PORT=<3000 + slot - 1> and
AUTOPILOT_SLOT=<slot> in its environment, so two runs of one project do not share a dev
server port.

Whatever the outcome - done, blocked, timeout, handoff-limit - the queue then pushes the
run's branch to origin (no PR, never a base branch, never forced, without the project's git
hooks), so a blocked run is visible on GitHub and its commits never exist on one Mac only.
A detached run with commits of its own gets the branch autopilot/<item> first.

Every git call ends after 10 minutes and every gh call after 2 (MISSION_CONTROL_GIT_TIMEOUT_MIN,
MISSION_CONTROL_GH_TIMEOUT_MIN, fractions allowed, 0 switches the limit off); a call that
reaches its limit is killed, counts as failed (exit 124) and is logged with its command.

Launch settings per item: model and effort from the plan header ("Model: sonnet | opus",
"Effort:"); MISSION_CONTROL_MODEL / _EFFORT in the environment win over the plan, the plan
wins over the env file. A sonnet run below xhigh runs at xhigh. Budget per attempt: 200 USD
for sonnet, 400 for opus, unless MISSION_CONTROL_BUDGET_USD is set. Wall clock per attempt:
480 minutes, unless MISSION_CONTROL_TIMEOUT_MIN is set.

Killing a run (timeout, stall, INT/TERM) targets the claude pid: SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after
10 s. A child process of a tool call (a dev server, a test runner) may survive that KILL.
INT and TERM (stop) end the run within a second while it waits for claude; a git or gh
command that is running finishes first.

Dependencies: git, gh, osascript (macOS notification). Slack is reached over HTTPS from here.
Test hooks: CLAUDE_BIN, GH_BIN, JIRA_BIN, JIRA_HOME, DOCKER_BIN, NVM_DIR,
MISSION_CONTROL_WATCH_MIN=0 (no stall check), MISSION_CONTROL_NO_NOTIFY=1.
Python 3 standard library only. Never prints a secret: the Slack webhook URL is only ever
held in a variable.
"""
